  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Homo Mimeticus shows strong signs of public visibility and sustained use. It has been downloaded 4,675 times through OAPEN, mentioned 13 times in Wikipedia, and appears in a teaching-related video discussion, while its scholarly citation record is still early with 4 OpenAlex citations.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Nidesh Lawtoo\u2019s Homo Mimeticus has already found a broad readership and a visible place in public knowledge resources. The record shows 4,675 downloads, suggesting active consultation, and 13 Wikipedia mentions, indicating that the book has entered wider explanatory and reference contexts beyond conventional scholarly circulation. It also has an open-access presence in OpenAIRE, which likely supports its reach.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book\u2019s thematic focus on imitation, politics, consciousness, aesthetics, and criticism is reflected in the emerging citation landscape. Its 4 OpenAlex citations are still an early signal, but they already come from a spread of formats and subjects, including an article on AI and clich\u00e9s, a chapter on psychiatric thought, a study of AI artists and creativity, and a dissertation on religion and mimetic thought. That mix suggests the book is being used in conversations that move across philosophy, AI ethics, psychiatry, creativity, and religion.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Teaching-related adoption is also visible: the book is discussed in a recorded dialogue hosted by the HOM Videos ERC Project Homo Mimeticus. In addition, the record links the book to the SDG area Quality Education, reinforcing its relevance for educational use and conceptual framing in teaching contexts.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The publication was supported by KU Leuven and the European Research Council, which situates it within a funded research program. Overall, the evidence points to a book with substantial public and pedagogical reach, alongside a modest but cross-disciplinary scholarly uptake that is beginning to accumulate.</p>",
